---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout
  title="Panel Layouts — Varve Docs"
  description="Draw comic panels, divide a page into panel grids with even gutters, join panels, and set reading order in Varve."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <main class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Panel Layouts
      </nav>
      <h1 class="site-page-title">Panel Layouts</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Comic panels in Varve are ordinary frames that clip their contents and have a solid border
        and a white interior. You can draw them one at a time with the Panel tool, or divide a page
        frame into a grid of panels in one step. There is no separate Comic workspace: the Panel
        tool is in the Design, Print, and Drawing toolbars.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Open the Panel tool</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Press <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>, or</li>
          <li>click <strong>Panel</strong> in the toolbar, next to Frame, or</li>
          <li>choose <strong>Arrange › Align › Panel Layouts…</strong>. This switches to the Panel tool and opens the Inspector's Properties tab.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The <strong>Comic (print)</strong> and <strong>Webtoon (vertical)</strong> workspace layouts
          start with the Panel tool already selected.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Draw a panel</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Drag on the canvas to draw a panel. Hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> for a square, or <kbd>Alt</kbd> to draw from the centre.</li>
          <li>Click without dragging to place a 400 × 300 panel.</li>
          <li>Artwork that the new panel fully covers is moved inside it, so you can sketch first and box it in afterwards.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Divide a page into panels</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>With the Panel tool active, select one frame, such as your page.</li>
          <li>In the Inspector, open <strong>Panel Layouts</strong> and choose a template.</li>
        </ol>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Splash</strong>: one full-page panel.</li>
          <li><strong>Two-up</strong>: two panels side by side.</li>
          <li><strong>Three-tier</strong>: three stacked rows.</li>
          <li><strong>Four-panel</strong>: a 2 × 2 grid.</li>
          <li><strong>Six-panel</strong>: three rows of two.</li>
          <li><strong>Nine-panel</strong>: a 3 × 3 grid.</li>
          <li><strong>Webtoon stack</strong>: four stacked panels with a much larger vertical gap for phone scrolling.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Gutters are even: each gutter, and the outer margin, is 4% of the frame's width. You can't
          set the gutter size in the template yet. Move or resize the panels afterwards if you need
          different spacing. If the frame already has artwork, each new panel gets its own copy of it.
          When that would use extra memory for raster layers, Varve shows an estimate first and asks
          you to choose <strong>Divide anyway</strong> or <strong>Cancel</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Join panels and set reading order</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Select two or more panels and choose <strong>Join selected panels</strong> in the Inspector or <strong>Arrange › Align › Join Panels</strong>. The artwork inside them is kept in place.</li>
          <li><strong>Arrange › Align › Renumber Panels (Left to Right)</strong> or <strong>(Right to Left)</strong> renames the panels in a frame in reading order. Use right to left for manga.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Panels are still frames. Move, resize, restyle, and export them the way you would any frame.
          Page and strip sizes come from Frame or New Document presets, not from panel templates.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Current limits</h2>
        <p>
          Templates are even grids only. There are no slanted or irregular borders, no adjustable
          gutter control, and no knife-style panel splitting. Templates are starting points, not
          publisher-specific guarantees. See <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/comic-lettering')}>Comic Lettering</a>
          for balloons and captions.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>
